# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TradeOS environment configuration
# Copy to `.env` and adjust. Everything below runs the app fully locally with
# no external accounts required beyond a local Postgres (built-in auth, no
# Stripe/Supabase needed) — see README.md's "Local dev" section.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Local database (Postgres). Run `docker compose up -d db` (or `npm run
# docker:up`) to start the bundled Postgres 16 container, which matches this
# default URL exactly. Using your own local Postgres instead? Point this at
# it (any user/db/password works, as long as it matches what you created).
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://tradeos:tradeos@localhost:5432/tradeos"

# Secret used to sign session JWTs for the built-in auth layer.
# REQUIRED in every environment (there is NO built-in fallback — a shared default
# would let anyone forge a login). It must be at least 32 characters; the app
# refuses to start signing/verifying sessions without a strong value here.
# The same secret also signs the anonymous rate-limit cookie (see TRUST_PROXY).
# Generate one:  openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_SECRET="change-me-generate-with-openssl-rand-base64-32"

# OPTIONAL dedicated key for encrypting stored broker API keys (AES-256-GCM).
# Leave unset and the key is derived from AUTH_SECRET (the original behavior).
# Set it on the FIRST deploy if you want login-signing and at-rest encryption
# managed separately — changing it later invalidates every stored broker key
# (users simply reconnect). Generate:  openssl rand -base64 32
# ENCRYPTION_SECRET=""

# Public app URL (used for links in reports, emails, etc.)
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL — leave blank for the local MVP. The app degrades gracefully and
# uses the built-in equivalents when these are unset.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Supabase (swap-in auth + storage). When set, the auth adapter can target it.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=""
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=""
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=""

# Stripe (subscription billing). When set, billing routes go live; otherwise
# the app runs in "trial/dev" billing mode with feature gating still enforced.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=""
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PRICE_PRO=""
# NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PRICE_ELITE=""

# AI coaching layer (Phase 4) — architecture-ready, disabled by default.
# AI_COACHING_ENABLED="false"
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""

# Error tracking (Sentry). Leave empty = OFF (dormant): nothing is sent and the
# SDK never initializes. Set it to your Sentry project DSN to switch on alerts.
# SENTRY_DSN=""

# Shared rate-limit store (scaling seam). Leave empty: a fast in-memory limiter
# is used, so limits count PER SERVER PROCESS. Setting this is the future switch
# for a shared Redis-backed limiter across multiple instances.
# REDIS_URL=""

# Trusted-proxy settings for rate limiting. The `x-forwarded-for` header is set
# by the client and can be spoofed, so by default it is IGNORED (safe for local
# dev). When you deploy BEHIND a proxy/load balancer (e.g. Railway), turn trust
# on so per-IP limits key off the real visitor address:
#   TRUST_PROXY="true"   — trust forwarded headers
#   PROXY_HOPS="1"       — how many proxies you run (take the Nth hop from the
#                          right of x-forwarded-for). Leave 1 for a single proxy.
# TRUST_PROXY="false"
# PROXY_HOPS="1"
#
# When TRUST_PROXY is OFF, anonymous (logged-out) rate limits can't see a real
# IP, so each browser is instead identified by a signed httpOnly cookie (HMAC'd
# with AUTH_SECRET) — every visitor gets their own login/register bucket rather
# than all sharing one. This is safe for local dev. In PRODUCTION the app logs a
# loud startup warning if TRUST_PROXY is still off, because behind a load
# balancer (e.g. Railway) you almost always want per-IP limits: set
# TRUST_PROXY="true".

# Scheduled broker auto-sync secret. REQUIRED for /api/cron/sync in EVERY
# environment — if it is unset the endpoint refuses to run (503). The scheduler
# must send it as:  Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>. Generate a strong one:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# CRON_SECRET=""

# Demo-data seeding guard (prisma/seed.ts). The demo account (demo@tradeos.app)
# is public-credentials and is NEVER seeded into a production database unless you
# explicitly opt in. If you do, a strong password is mandatory.
# SEED_DEMO="true"                 — force-seed the demo account in production
# SEED_DEMO_PASSWORD="<12+ chars>" — required strong password when SEED_DEMO=true in prod
